.Dd October 6, 2026
.Dt PTRACE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ptrace
.Nd trace a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/ptrace.h
.Ft long
.Fn ptrace "int request" "pid_t pid" "void *addr" "void *data"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm ,
number 26 on i386 and 101 on x86-64, lets one process, the tracer, stop the threads of another, its
tracees, read and change their memory and registers, and resume them, as
GDB and strace do.
.Fa pid
is a thread ID.
The tracer is a process: any thread of it may make the requests and wait
for the tracees.
On Linux the tracer is the thread that attached, and a request from
another thread of its process is
.Er ESRCH .
.Pp
A tracee stops for its tracer in a
.Em ptrace stop ,
which the tracer learns of from
.Xr wait 2
with the status
.Dv WIFSTOPPED ,
and from a
.Dv SIGCHLD
with
.Dv CLD_TRAPPED .
The tracer's wait reports each stop once, and reports a traced thread that
is not the first of its process only with
.Dv __WALL
or
.Dv __WCLONE .
The end of a traced process whose parent is another process is reported
to the tracer first, and the report lets the process go: its parent then
receives
.Dv SIGCHLD
and may reap it.
A tracer that ends lets its tracees go, and the parent of one whose
process has ended is told of the end the same way.
The end of a tracee sends the tracer a
.Dv SIGCHLD
too, with
.Dv CLD_EXITED
and the exit status, or
.Dv CLD_KILLED
or
.Dv CLD_DUMPED
and the signal, as
.Xr wait 2
reports the end.
The stops are:
.Bl -tag -width "signal-delivery stop"
.It signal-delivery stop
A signal the tracee is about to act on, other than
.Dv SIGKILL ,
with the signal in the status; the signal the tracer resumes it with is
the one acted on, 0 for none, and a blocked one stays pending.
.It group stop
The stop of the tracee's process by a stop signal, after the
signal-delivery stop for it; the tracer may resume the thread while the
process stays stopped.
With
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
it is reported with
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP
in bits 16 to 23 of the status.
.It event stop
.Dv SIGTRAP
with an event in bits 16 to 23, when the options ask for it:
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_FORK ,
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_VFORK
and
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_CLONE
after a
.Xr fork 2 ,
a
.Xr vfork 2
or
.Xr clone 2
by the tracee, whose new child or thread is then traced too and first
stops with
.Dv SIGSTOP ,
or under
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
with
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP
and
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
as strace's
.Fl f
waits for it;
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_VFORK_DONE
when the vfork child lets the tracee go;
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_EXEC
after an
.Xr execve 2 ;
and
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_EXIT
as the thread begins to end.
.Dv PTRACE_GETEVENTMSG
reads the new child's ID, the thread's ID before the exec, or the exit
status.
Without
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEEXEC ,
an
.Xr execve 2
sends a tracee
.Dv SIGTRAP
unless it was attached with
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE ,
as on Linux; a seized tracee then has no stop for the exec.
.It syscall stop
At the entry and at the exit of each system call, after
.Dv PTRACE_SYSCALL ,
with
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
or
.Dv SIGTRAP No | 0x80
with
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACESYSGOOD .
At the entry EAX holds
.Er -ENOSYS
and orig_eax the call's number; at the exit EAX holds the result.
A tracer that changes orig_eax at the entry changes the call, as on
Linux: the number it leaves there is the call run, one of no call returns
.Er ENOSYS ,
and -1 runs none, the call returning what the tracer left in EAX.
A tracee
.Dv SIGKILL
reaches during the entry stop runs no call, as on Linux.
The arguments are the registers as the tracer leaves them.
.El
.Pp
The requests are:
.Bl -tag -width "PTRACE_GET_THREAD_AREA"
.It Dv PTRACE_TRACEME
The caller's parent becomes its tracer.
.It Dv PTRACE_ATTACH , PTRACE_SEIZE
The caller's process becomes the tracer of
.Fa pid .
.Dv PTRACE_ATTACH
sends it
.Dv SIGSTOP ;
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
stops nothing and takes the options in
.Fa data .
A thread whose process a stop signal has already stopped is reported to
the tracer at once in a group stop, as on Linux, and its process stays
stopped.
The caller needs a process whose every live thread is privileged, or a
real user and group ID equal to each of the target's three, a dumpable
target and a target that is not privileged, as Linux asks of a tracer
without
.Dv CAP_SYS_PTRACE :
a root thread that gave up its capabilities
.Pq see Xr capget 2
cannot trace one that has them.
A process traces as a whole, since each of its threads may make the
tracer's requests, so it is as privileged as its least privileged live
thread; Linux makes the attaching thread alone the tracer.
For the same reason a privileged thread cannot make its tracer, with
.Dv PTRACE_TRACEME ,
a parent process with a live thread that is not privileged.
Such a caller cannot attach to a thread inside
.Xr execve 2 ,
which chooses the IDs of the new image by the tracer the thread has: for
a caller of its own without effective user ID 0, it ignores set-ID bits
under such a tracer.
Linux makes the attach wait for the exec to end.
A thread of the caller's own process, init, a kernel thread and a thread
already traced cannot be attached.
.It Dv PTRACE_PEEKTEXT , PTRACE_PEEKDATA , PTRACE_POKETEXT , PTRACE_POKEDATA
Read the word at
.Fa addr
of the tracee's memory into the word
.Fa data
points to, or write
.Fa data
there.
Missing pages are brought in, and a write reaches a read-only private page,
such as program text, through a copy of its own that stays read-only, so a
breakpoint changes this process alone; a page of no mapping, of a
.Dv PROT_NONE
one, or of a read-only shared one for a write, is
.Er EIO ,
and so is every address of a tracee an exec after the attach made not
dumpable, for a requesting thread that is not privileged, as on Linux,
whose check reads the credentials of the attach where this one reads
the requesting thread's own.
.It Dv PTRACE_PEEKUSER , PTRACE_POKEUSER
A word of the architecture's
.Vt struct user
at byte
.Fa addr :
the registers of
.Vt user_regs_struct
from 0, the debug registers
.Va u_debugreg
from byte 252 on i386 or 848 on x86-64, with four-byte or eight-byte
words respectively; the floating-point valid mark reads 1 and the other
fields 0.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETREGS , PTRACE_SETREGS
The
.Vt user_regs_struct
at
.Fa data ,
68 bytes on i386 and 216 on x86-64.
A tracer changes only the flags a program may change; the data segment
registers take a null selector, the user data selector or the TLS entry's
on i386, and CS and SS only the ones they have.
On x86-64 DS, ES, FS and GS read the thread's selectors and take a null
selector, 3, the user data selector or the user code selector, which
Linux also takes, and the kernel loads the base words after them; a write
of any other selector fails with
.Er EIO .
orig_rax is kept in 32 bits and reads back sign-extended, where Linux
keeps all 64.
An orig_eax of -1 keeps an interrupted system call from being restarted,
set at its exit stop or at the signal-delivery stop that follows; at the
exit stop so does a result in EAX other than
.Er -EINTR .
.It Dv PTRACE_GETFPREGS , PTRACE_SETFPREGS
The
.Vt user_i387_struct :
on i386 108 bytes in the
.Ic fnsave
layout, and on x86-64 the 512-byte
.Ic fxsave
image.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETFPXREGS , PTRACE_SETFPXREGS
The 512-byte
.Ic fxsave
image;
.Er EIO
on a processor without FXSR, and
.Er EINVAL
for an MXCSR the processor refuses.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETREGSET , PTRACE_SETREGSET
The register set that
.Fa addr
names by the type of the core note that has it, to or from the buffer of
the
.Vt struct iovec
at
.Fa data ,
whose length becomes the bytes moved:
.Dv NT_PRSTATUS
(1), the
.Vt user_regs_struct ;
.Dv NT_PRFPREG
(2), the
.Vt user_i387_struct ,
or on x86-64 the
.Ic fxsave64
image;
.Dv NT_PRXFPREG
(0x46e62b7f), on i386 the
.Ic fxsave
image; and
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE
(0x202), the XSAVE image, as large as leaf 0xD of
.Ic cpuid
gives for the components the kernel enabled, with XCR0 in the first eight
of its 48 software bytes at byte 464, where GDB reads which components the
image has.
The length must be a multiple of 4, or of 8 for
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE
and on x86-64, and is cut to the set's size; a get of less gives the set's
first bytes.
A set of the registers takes as many as the length holds, in order, and
one of the i386
.Dv NT_PRFPREG
the bytes the length covers.
The
.Ic fxsave
images and the XSAVE image are set whole:
.Er EINVAL
for less of an
.Ic fxsave
image and
.Er EFAULT
for less of the XSAVE image, as on Linux.
A set XSAVE image becomes the initial state with the components its
XSTATE_BV marks in use, and MXCSR, copied from it;
.Er EINVAL
for an XSTATE_BV that names a component XCR0 lacks, a header whose other
bytes are not zero, or a reserved MXCSR bit.
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE
on a processor whose state the kernel keeps without XSAVE, and
.Dv NT_PRXFPREG
on one without FXSR, are
.Er ENODEV ;
another type is
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv PTRACE_CONT , PTRACE_SYSCALL , PTRACE_SINGLESTEP
Resume the tracee with the signal
.Fa data ,
0 for none; after a stop that is not a signal-delivery stop the signal is
sent afresh after a syscall stop and dropped after any other.
.Dv PTRACE_SYSCALL
stops it at the next system call's entry or exit, and
.Dv PTRACE_SINGLESTEP
after one instruction, with
.Dv SIGTRAP
and
.Dv TRAP_TRACE .
A step over a system call stops at its exit, with its result available,
before the next user instruction.
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACESYSGOOD
does not change that signal.
.It Dv PTRACE_KILL
Send the tracee
.Dv SIGKILL .
.It Dv PTRACE_DETACH
Resume the tracee with the signal
.Fa data
and stop tracing it; its debug registers are cleared.
A tracee whose process is stopped then stops with it, as on Linux.
.It Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
Stop a tracee attached with
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
with
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP
and
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
at its next return to user mode.
A tracee blocked in a system call that a signal would interrupt is
interrupted for the stop, as a stop signal would interrupt it: a call that
is restarted after a stop and a continue is restarted when the tracer
resumes the tracee, and one that is not, such as
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
fails with
.Er EINTR ,
where Linux restarts it.
A tracee that
.Dv PTRACE_LISTEN
keeps in its stop makes the new stop before executing another user
instruction, including after repeated listen and interrupt requests.
.It Dv PTRACE_LISTEN
For a seized tracee in a
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP ,
a group stop's or, as Linux checks only the event, one
.Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
made outside a group stop:
the tracee stays stopped, as a stopped process does, until its process's
stop ends with
.Dv SIGCONT
or begins again, when it makes a new
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP ,
with
.Dv SIGTRAP
after the end or the stop signal after a new stop, or when the tracer
asks for one with
.Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT ;
at once when such a change came before the request.
A seized tracee not listening makes the same stop when its tracer next
resumes it.
.It Dv PTRACE_SETOPTIONS
Set the supported option bits below.
An unsupported bit anywhere in the native word is
.Er EINVAL ,
for this request and
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE ,
and leaves the tracing state unchanged.
.Pp
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACESYSGOOD ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEFORK ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEVFORK ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACECLONE ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEEXEC ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEVFORKDONE ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEEXIT ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACESECCOMP ,
for the stop of a seccomp filter's
.Dv SECCOMP_RET_TRACE ,
and
.Dv PTRACE_O_EXITKILL ,
which sends the tracees
.Dv SIGKILL
when the tracer ends; every tracee is detached then.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETEVENTMSG
The event's message, into the word at
.Fa data .
.It Dv PTRACE_GETSIGINFO , PTRACE_SETSIGINFO
The 128-byte
.Vt siginfo_t
of the stop.
.It Dv PTRACE_GET_THREAD_AREA , PTRACE_SET_THREAD_AREA
The TLS descriptor of entry
.Fa addr ,
6, 7 or 8, as a
.Vt user_desc ,
as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes it.
The kernel has one TLS entry, 6.
Entries 7 and 8 read as Linux reads an unused entry, and a write to them
must leave them unused.
.El
.Pp
Every request but
.Dv PTRACE_TRACEME ,
.Dv PTRACE_ATTACH ,
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE ,
.Dv PTRACE_KILL
and
.Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
needs the tracee in a ptrace stop.
.Ss Debug registers
DR0 to DR3 hold breakpoint addresses, DR6 the status of the last debug
exception, and DR7 the control bits, which the kernel loads when the
tracee runs.
DR7 may enable a breakpoint only on an address aligned to its length of
1, 2 or 4 bytes, or 8 on x86-64, whose bytes are all user addresses, from
0x02000000 to 0xbfffffff on i686 and from 0x10000 to 0x7fffffffefff on
x86-64, an execute breakpoint only of length 1, and no I/O breakpoint,
general-detect bit or reserved bit; a value against these is
.Er EINVAL .
A breakpoint that fires sends
.Dv SIGTRAP
with
.Dv TRAP_HWBKPT ,
also when the kernel touches the address in a copy for the tracee.
A hit deferred in the kernel is posted as that signal before the kernel
next checks the tracee's pending signals, so it interrupts an
interruptible wait subject to its signal mask and disposition, and
.Xr rt_sigtimedwait 2
or a
.Xr signalfd 2
read can take it.
A hit on a user instruction that the processor delays into the
system-call entry, as after a watched
.Li mov ss ,
ends an interruptible wait in that call whatever the signal mask, is not
taken by a signal wait, and is forced as a user-mode hit is, through a
blocked
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
so the tracee stops at the call's return.
An Intel processor reports such a hit with no breakpoint bit in DR6, and
the tracee's DR6 then shows none.
Thus an infinite
.Xr poll 2
whose descriptor the kernel reads through a watchpoint stops for the
tracer and returns
.Er EINTR
after the tracer suppresses the signal and resumes it.
When the tracer ends, any breakpoint still loaded on the processor
running the tracee is disabled at its next hit, switch or user return.
That stale hit sends no hardware-breakpoint signal; a simultaneous single
step still sends its signal.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0, or the negative error number of the raw call; the C libraries' wrappers
return -1 and set
.Va errno ,
and return the word for the three peek requests.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/ptracemusl.c
traces a child that stops itself, reads and writes its memory directly and
through
.Pa /proc/PID/mem ,
reads its registers and floating-point state, steps an instruction, stops
it at a breakpoint in its read-only text and moves it back, stops it at
the entry and exit of a system call and after an
.Xr execve 2 ,
changes one call into another and has another run none at their entry
stops, cancels the restart of an interrupted read at its exit stop, sets
and reads a debug register, has a breakpoint on a kernel address
refused and one ending at the last user address taken, attaches to and
detaches from a running process, seizes a stopped one, interrupts a
seized one blocked in a read, and reads the
.Dv SIGCHLD
of a tracee
.Dv SIGKILL
ends.
.Pa tests/xsaveprobe.c
reads a stopped child's YMM registers through
.Dv PTRACE_GETREGSET
with
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE ,
writes one through
.Dv PTRACE_SETREGSET ,
and finds it in the child.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A buffer at
.Fa data ,
or the buffer of its
.Vt struct iovec ,
cannot be read or written, or a
.Dv PTRACE_SETREGSET
of
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE
has less than the whole image.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A debug register, an MXCSR, an XSAVE header, a TLS descriptor or entry,
an option, or a register set the kernel does not take, or a register set's
length that is not a multiple of its unit.
.It Bq Er EIO
An unknown request, a
.Vt struct user
offset out of range or not a word's, a register value the kernel refuses,
a signal past 64, a memory address no mapping allows, or
.Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
or
.Dv PTRACE_LISTEN
for a tracee that was not seized, and
.Dv PTRACE_LISTEN
for one in any stop but a
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP .
.It Bq Er ENODEV
A register set the processor lacks:
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE
without XSAVE, or
.Dv NT_PRXFPREG
without FXSR.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel has no room for the buffer of a register set.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The attach is not allowed, the target is inside
.Xr execve 2
and the caller's effective user ID is not 0, or the caller is already
traced.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No such thread, one the caller's process does not trace, or one not in a
ptrace stop, which for a request that reads from
.Fa data
is judged again after the read.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr set_thread_area 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
